WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.440
Hello everyone, welcome to the lecture on 3D computer vision.

00:03.440 --> 00:08.640
And today we are going to talk about three view geometry from points and or lines.

00:09.760 --> 00:15.360
And hopefully by the end of today's lecture, you'll be able to derive the trifocal tensor

00:15.360 --> 00:20.480
constraint from points, line, image correspondences of three views.

00:21.040 --> 00:26.640
And you will be able to describe the homography relations between two views.

00:26.640 --> 00:33.120
In particular, we'll look at how to derive this homography relations from the trifocal tensor.

00:33.920 --> 00:39.920
Next, we'll look at how to extract the three view epipoles and epipolar lines from the trifocal

00:39.920 --> 00:46.720
tensor. And we'll also look at how to decompose the trifocal tensor into the camera matrices,

00:46.720 --> 00:53.040
as well as the fundamental matrices that relates the three views. Finally, we'll compute

00:53.680 --> 00:59.200
the trifocal tensor from image point-like correspondences of three views.

01:01.040 --> 01:07.360
And of course, as usual, I didn't invent any of today's material. I took most of the content from

01:07.360 --> 01:14.800
chapter 15 and 16 of Richard Tuckley and Andrew Zizerman's textbook, Multiview Geometry in Computer

01:14.800 --> 01:20.960
Vision, which I strongly encourage every one of you to read these two chapters after today's

01:20.960 --> 01:28.240
lecture. And if you are interested, please also read chapter 8 of Mahi's textbook, An Invitation

01:28.240 --> 01:39.120
to 3D Vision. So in the previous lecture, we have learned the fundamental matrix and its role in

01:39.120 --> 01:45.840
relating or describing the geometric relations between two views. So today we'll look at the

01:45.840 --> 01:52.160
trifocal tensor, which is analogous to the fundamental matrix, but it's meant to relate

01:52.160 --> 01:59.040
the geometry of three different views. In particular, it encapsulates all the geometrical

01:59.040 --> 02:05.360
or projective relation between these three views that are independent of the scene structure. What

02:05.360 --> 02:12.480
this means is that given these three views, given the three views, as long as we know

02:12.480 --> 02:20.000
the image correspondences, it could be a point, a point-point, or it could be a mixture of point

02:20.000 --> 02:28.880
and lines. For example, we will be able to relate and compute the trifocal tensor that relates these

02:29.440 --> 02:36.560
three views, and then we'll be able to decompose this trifocal tensor into the geometric

02:36.560 --> 02:41.360
formation of camera projection matrices, fundamental matrices, etc.

